1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Listen to part of a lecture in a United States history class.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,432
In the days before telephones, radio,

3
00:00:06,432 --> 00:00:11,229
and television, the only network of public communication that could reach farmers was the

4
00:00:11,229 --> 00:00:11,530
mail.

5
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:14,200
But this wasn’t the mail as we know it today.

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,850
At that time, in the early nineteenth century, mail delivery was uneven

7
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,012
and widely scattered.

8
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:25,050
In fact, many people living in rural areas got no mail at all.

9
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:29,319
In the early decades of the century, mail carriers were privately employed – they

10
00:00:29,319 --> 00:00:31,290
did not work for the government.

11
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:32,335
Many years later,

12
00:00:32,335 --> 00:00:37,213
when the government finally took charge of delivering mail, it was mostly in cities

13
00:00:37,213 --> 00:00:40,000
that mail got delivered to people’s homes.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,884
So farmers still had a problem – they had to go to a post

15
00:00:43,884 --> 00:00:45,378
office to collect their mail,

16
00:00:45,378 --> 00:00:47,768
which, by the way, wasn’t always nearby.

17
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:52,250
Farmers’ requests to have mail brought to their homes were at first met with outrage.

18
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:57,753
What could be more ridiculous, many urban residents asked, than paying government employees to

19
00:00:57,753 --> 00:01:01,290
travel miles across the countryside with an occasional letter?

20
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:09,190
Nevertheless, farmers’ organizations succeeded in convincing the United States Congress that farmers needed mail delivery.

21
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:14,736
Finally in 1891 rural free delivery, known as RFD, came into being.

22
00:01:14,736 --> 00:01:22,130
In a sense, rural free delivery was the most important communications revolution in United States history.

23
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:28,050
Rural Americans were not lifted out of the relatively isolated communities they lived in.

24
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:32,709
Because of rural free delivery, there now ran a highway to the world from

25
00:01:32,709 --> 00:01:34,040
every farmer’s doorstep.
